-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                        MCI file maker by Ed Mackey.
                               version 1.3
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

This program is freely distributable.  Upload it to BBSs, put it on PD
disks, etc.

PLEASE NOTE:
    This program was written in an afternoon and a half.  It is by no means
    one of my better pieces of programming; it's just a little Freeware
    utility hacked together for some friends and a BBS.

    Version 1.1 was created after about 20 minutes of fiddling with v1.0's
    source code.  I added the "options" menu, with one option:  You can
    now specify whether MCI maker will save an MCI "clear screen" command
    at the top of saved MCI files.  This option is affected by both
    LOAD and NEW operations.

    Version 1.2 was created after about 10 minutes of fiddling with v1.1's
    source code.  It now resets the background color to zero at the end of
    each line, and changes it back to the current background color at the
    start of the next line.  This is to prevent weird CNet color problems.
    (Also, the width of the screen was reduced from 79 to 78 by request,
    for similar reasons involving indenting).

    Version 1.3 added another afternoon of programming time to this little
    project.  It now has an interlace mode that increases the maximum number
    of lines from 23 to 48 for those tall graphics.  It also has an option
    to prevent users from aborting a particular screen as it comes on the
    screen.  It even gained a "Reverse video" option, although the checkmark
    next to this option is meaningless -- every time the option is selected,
    the foreground and background colors are exchanged (except for the
    highlight bit) so no actual /R commands are needed or used in the output.
    I also got a request to drop the "#?.mci" from the Show gadget of the
    file requester.  It now shows all files in the dir by default.

FUNCTION:
    The only function of this program is to generate color screens for BBSs
    that use the MCI textfile format (like CNet).  It doesn't even come close
    to supporting the full MCI standard; that is not its function.

This editor only edits one screen at a time.  If you try to load a textfile
that has more than a screenful of text in it, you will see the message
WARNING: FILE WAS TRUNCATED.  If you then save this file, you will lose
all the text that did not fit on the screen.  ALSO, this editor only
supports the simplest of MCI color commands.  If there are other MCI
commands in the file you load, they are likely to be ignored or discarded.

Basically I recommend you only LOAD files that were created and SAVEd
by this editor.  That way you cannot lose any data.

Instructions:
    Once in the program, click the mouse to get past the info screen.
    Type on the keys to enter text into the editor.
    Most of the functions are in the pull-down menus and are obvious.

** Here are the non-obvious things: **

This program looks for an 8-point font called myibm.font.  If found, it will
use this font to simulate the IBM character set in its display.  Note that
the LIBS:diskfont.library must also be present in order for any disk-based
fonts to be used by this (or any) Amiga program.  If MCImaker is unable to
access myibm.font for any reason, topaz 8 will be used instead.

* Unfortunately, I could not include myibm.font with this archive, as the font
  was mostly a rip-off from the font that came with my bridgeboard.  If anyone
  has a freely distributable IBM font, please send it to me!

This program requires the req.library version 1 or greater.

The normal cursor diamond should work under 2.0, but it might not under 1.3,
due to differences in RAWKEY handling by the 2 OSs.  However, the cursor
diamond embedded in the numeric pad works under all OSs.  Clicking the mouse
also moves the cursor.

The "home" and "end" keys in the numeric keypad take you to the start or end
of a line. The "PgUp" and "PgDn" keys in the pad take you to the top or
bottom of the screen. Both "del" keys (one in the pad and one near HELP)
delete characters to the right. The backspace key deletes characters to the
left. The "ins" key in the pad shifts characters to the right to make room
for new ones.

The middle key in the pad ("5") updates the foreground and background colors
of the character under the cursor, and then moves the cursor one character
to the right. -- You can re-color whole lines with this without having to
re-type everything!

To enter actual numbers, use the numbers on the main keyboard, not the
numeric pad (because of the functions listed above).

The backslash character ("\") is automatically doubled in the saved MCI file,
as the MCI specification requires.  In other words, you can use the backslash
all you want and not worry about it conflicting with MCI commands.

The TAB key centers a line of text.

Everything else is in the menus.  Have fun!
                                                 --Ed.

